{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs",
  "item": "impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "The provider overrides parse with a cached snapshot. Selected-source\n reads must deliver and fingerprint that snapshot without fetching again;\n this trait-required callback rejects a refetch. Primitive provider reads\n are covered separately, and executing this guard would fail this test."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` is correct.\n\n- **Placement:** It sits on an explicit function with a body, inside fenced commentary. That is a permitted form for a function-level coverage exception.\n- **Substance:** The commentary gives a real reason, not just difficulty. `SourceProvider::read` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#trait[SourceProvider]` is a required method with no default. `parse` has a default that calls `read`. `CachedProvider` overrides `parse` to return a cached snapshot through `parse_snapshot`. `read` therefore exists only to satisfy the trait. It deliberately returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"must not refetch\")`, so executing it would mean a refetch happened and would fail the test.\n- **Agreement with source:** The commentary matches the source. The test `cached_selected_source_reads_record_exact_delivered_contents_and_errors_add_no_input` uses `CachedProvider` with a graph whose current source differs. It asserts that the recorded fingerprint equals the cached snapshot text `fn observed() { call(); }`. That assertion only passes if `read` is never called.\n- **Other roles:** The item carries no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference, so the citation is not redundant.",
